  <h2>Gotchas for bash users</h2>
  <ul class="gotchas">
    <li><strong>Output is objects, not text.</strong> <code>Get-ChildItem | Select-Object Name, Length</code> picks properties. You rarely need <code>awk</code> or <code>cut</code>. See <a href="/guides/pipelines/">Pipelines</a>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>ls -la</code> does not work.</strong> <code>ls</code> is an alias for <code>Get-ChildItem</code>, which has its own parameters. Use <code>Get-ChildItem -Force</code> to include hidden files.</li>
    <li><strong><code>rm -rf</code> becomes <code>Remove-Item -Recurse -Force</code>.</strong> Add <code>-WhatIf</code> first. There is no recycle bin.</li>
    <li><strong><code>curl</code> and <code>wget</code> are aliases on Windows PowerShell 5.1</strong> for <code>Invoke-WebRequest</code>, so flags like <code>-O</code> fail. PowerShell 7 removed those aliases, so <code>curl</code> runs the real curl.exe.</li>
    <li><strong>Comparison uses words.</strong> <code>-eq</code>, <code>-ne</code>, <code>-gt</code>, <code>-lt</code>. A bare <code>&gt;</code> is redirection and will create a file. See <a href="/guides/operators/">Operators</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Environment variables</strong> are <code>$env:PATH</code>, not <code>$PATH</code>. Set one with <code>$env:NAME = 'value'</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code></strong> only work in PowerShell 7. In 5.1, use <code>;</code> or <code>if ($?) { ... }</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Case-insensitive by default.</strong> <code>-eq</code>, <code>-like</code>, and <code>Select-String</code> ignore case. Use <code>-ceq</code>, <code>-clike</code>, or <code>-CaseSensitive</code> when it matters.</li>
    <li><strong>Strings:</strong> double quotes expand <code>$variables</code>, single quotes do not. The escape character is the backtick <code>`</code>, not the backslash.</li>
  </ul>

